# Fontly generator rules

## Code point handling. Get this exactly right.

Most of these styles live above U+FFFF, which means each character is two
UTF-16 code units in JavaScript. Naive code breaks in two ways and both
must be handled explicitly.

- Never use charCodeAt, and never index a string with square brackets when
  mapping. Iterate with for (const ch of str), or spread the string, or use
  codePointAt, so a styled character is never split in half.
- Never use str.length as the user-facing character count. It reports two
  per styled letter. Report both numbers with plain-language labels, for
  example "12 characters, 24 on X/Twitter". The first is the code point
  count, the second is the UTF-16 length. This is a genuine competitor
  gap. Make it visible, not hidden in a tooltip.

Build the mapping as an offset calculation, not a hand-typed lookup table.
Each block in Mathematical Alphanumeric Symbols holds uppercase A to Z at
a base, then lowercase a to z twenty-six code points later.

## Style record shape

Each entry in styles.ts carries an id, a display name, a category, an
uppercase base, a lowercase base, a substitutions map of code point
overrides, a digits field where null means digits pass through unchanged,
and a risk field which is one of safe, uneven, or check-old-devices.
risk is a compatibility claim governed by the honesty rules. It must never
be assigned by assumption and never carry the same value on every style.
Only two grounds are valid: a Unicode fact checkable without a device, where
a style drawn from more than one Unicode block is uneven because font fallback
resolves per block, or real device testing at stage 10. Anything else stays
unset, and unset is a correct answer rather than a gap waiting to be filled.

## The six cursive styles, with exact bases

- bold-script, Bold Script, uppercase U+1D4D0, lowercase U+1D4EA
- script, Classic Script, uppercase U+1D49C, lowercase U+1D4B6
- bold-italic, Bold Italic, uppercase U+1D468, lowercase U+1D482
- italic, Italic Script, uppercase U+1D434, lowercase U+1D44E
- sans-italic, Handwriting, uppercase U+1D608, lowercase U+1D622
- sans-bold-italic, Slanted, uppercase U+1D63C, lowercase U+1D656

## The substitution gaps that every cheap generator gets wrong

Unicode did not duplicate letters it had already encoded elsewhere, so
some slots inside these blocks are reserved and empty. Calculating the
offset without overriding them makes those letters render as blanks or
tofu.

Classic Script needs eleven overrides, all from Letterlike Symbols:
B is U+212C, E is U+2130, F is U+2131, H is U+210B, I is U+2110,
L is U+2112, M is U+2133, R is U+211B, lowercase e is U+212F,
lowercase g is U+210A, lowercase o is U+2134.

Italic Script needs one override: lowercase h is U+210E.

After implementing this, print all fifty-two characters of every style to
the console so they can be checked against these lists. Do not say it
works. Show the output.

## Digits and unconvertible input

Cursive digits do not exist in Unicode. There is no cursive five. For
every cursive style, digits pass through unchanged, and the tool shows a
small persistent note on those cards saying numbers stay plain, so nobody
thinks the tool is broken. Other styles such as circled and bold do have
digits, which is why digits is a field and not a hardcoded assumption.

Anything the generator cannot convert, including accented letters and any
non-Latin script, passes through unchanged. Never substitute a lookalike
character as a guess.

## Performance rules for the tool

An earlier prototype re-rendered one hundred and thirty style cards on
every keystroke and janked badly on mobile. Do not repeat that.

- Debounce the input at roughly 120 ms before re-rendering.
- Use an IntersectionObserver so only cards currently in the viewport get
  recalculated. Offscreen cards recalculate when they scroll into view.
- Build the card list once at page load. On updates, set textContent on
  the existing output nodes. Never rebuild the DOM, never use innerHTML in
  a loop, never recreate card elements.
- Render the card list server-side in Astro so the page is useful before
  any JavaScript runs. JavaScript only wires up interactivity.
- No layout shift. Cards must have their final dimensions before JS runs.

## Tool features, in build order

Build these in this order. Do not start a later one before an earlier one
is correct and reviewed.

1. One input field above the fold with a real label. Prefill it with the
   page's own test phrase rather than leaving it empty, for example
   "Soda Pop 40" on the bubble page and "Wumpus" on the Discord page.
2. Category sections driven by toolCategories in the page frontmatter.
   Each category gets a visible heading with its real name. No generic
   labels such as "Style 47" anywhere.
3. A card per style showing the real style name, the user's text rendered
   in it, and a copy button.
4. Copy via the async Clipboard API with a document.execCommand fallback.
   Announce success in the aria-live region.
5. The dual character counter described above.
6. A quiet per-card risk indicator driven by the risk field. Render it only
   when a style actually carries a value. No value means no badge, never a
   default badge.
7. Case transform buttons: UPPER, lower, Title, aLt. Pure string
   operations on the input before styling.
8. A search box that filters the visible style cards by name and category.
   Provide a real empty state. Filtering must not cause layout shift.
9. Favourites, toggled by a star on each card, and a recently-used list.
   Both persist in localStorage. Render both section shells server-side
   with their empty states, so reading localStorage on load causes no
   layout shift.
10. Platform previews: Instagram bio, Discord nickname, X display name and
    TikTok display name, showing the user's text in a chosen style inside
    a realistic frame. No follower counts, no like counts, no timestamps,
    no invented engagement numbers of any kind. Frame chrome only.
11. A decorated section, clearly labelled and separate from the plain
    styles, where a style is a base alphabet wrapped in symbols. Never
    present a wrapped variant as if it were a distinct alphabet.

## Deferred until every character is verified correct

Do not build these until the generator has been checked against the code
point lists above and the owner has confirmed it.

- The combo builder: base style, plus an optional separator between
  letters, plus an optional wrapper around the text, with a live preview
  and a copy button. When it is built, the dual character counter must be
  visible inside the combo panel, because separators multiply the
  character count and silently break platform bio limits. Label combo
  output as decoration, not as a font.
- A random style button.
- Keyboard shortcuts. If they are ever added, document only the ones
  actually tested.

The reason for deferring is ordering, not dislike. The earlier prototype
shipped a combo builder and a random button while its characters were
still wrong.
